const e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"LR_E7A_Q001","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":false,"question_text":"In a day, how many times does the lorry start from A and what is the unused capacity of the lorry per day?","options":{"A":"4 times and 100 units.","B":"5 times and 200 units.","C":"3 times and zero units.","D":"5 times and zero units."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"3 times and zero units.","solution_text":"Total daily requirement = 78+61+26+42+34+59 = 300 units. Lorry capacity = 100 units/trip, so minimum trips = 300/100 = 3. Since 3 trips x 100 = 300 exactly matches total demand, all capacity is used up across the day, leaving zero unused capacity.","entity_count":6,"variables":1,"constraints":2,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"low","solving_methodologies":["arithmetic_computation"],"question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90,"question_image":"QIMG_LR_E7A_Q001","passage":"A is the reserve station from which a lorry can take 100 units of liquid at a time to be supplied to B, C, D, E, F and G, whose requirements per day are 78, 61, 26, 42, 34 and 59 units respectively. Every day a lorry starts at A and supplies the requirements. In this process it moves back and forth from A to the other places and the location of all places is shown in the diagram below."},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q002","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":false,"question_text":"What is the minimum number of stones required to weigh an item which weighs 68.892 g?","options":{"A":"6","B":"5","C":"3","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"68.892 g can be built up using one stone from each of the four available ranges (a tens-place stone, a units/half-integer stone, a hundredths-range stone and a thousandths-range stone), so the minimum number of stones needed is 4.","entity_count":4,"variables":1,"constraints":4,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["place_value_decomposition"],"question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120,"question_table":{"headers":["Stone Set","Number of Stones","Weights (g)","Interval (g)"],"rows":[["Set 1",9,"1.001 - 1.009",0.001],["Set 2",49,"1.01 - 1.49",0.01],["Set 3",19,"0.5 - 9.5",0.5],["Set 4",9,"10 - 90",10]]},"passage":"The following sets of weighing stones are available, as shown in the table below."},{"set_id":"LR_E7A_S3_5","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Ravi, a retail dealer of Air-Tel prepaid cards, asked his brother Gopi to buy cards of denominations Rs.200, Rs.700, Rs.1,000, Rs.1,500 and Rs.2,000. He asked Gopi to buy five cards each of exactly three of the above denominations and six cards each of the remaining denominations. Gopi forgot which denominations he was supposed to buy five and which six of each, but the wholesale dealer could figure out how many cards of each denomination were required as Ravi had sent an amount of Rs.30,000, which was the exact amount required to buy the cards of Ravi's choice.","setup_solution":"Total value of all 5 denominations = 200+700+1000+1500+2000 = 5400. If A = sum of the 3 denominations bought 5 each and B = sum of the 2 bought 6 each, then 5A+6B=30000 and A+B=5400, giving A=2400. The only 3 denominations summing to 2400 are Rs.200, Rs.700 and Rs.1,500 (bought 5 each); the remaining Rs.1,000 and Rs.2,000 were bought 6 each.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":2,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7A_Q003","question_text":"What is the ratio of the total number of cards of denominations Rs.200 and Rs.2,000 to those of all other cards purchased by Gopi?","options":{"A":"10 : 17","B":"16 : 11","C":"17 : 11","D":"11 : 16"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"11 : 16","solution_text":"Cards of Rs.200 = 5, cards of Rs.2,000 = 6, total = 11. Total cards overall = 5x3 + 6x2 = 27, so all other cards = 27-11 = 16. Ratio = 11:16.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q004","question_text":"What is the total value of all those cards of which five each were bought?","options":{"A":"14400","B":"12000","C":"18000","D":"16000"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"12000","solution_text":"Total value of the three denominations bought 5 each = 5x(200+700+1500) = 5x2400 = Rs.12,000.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":75},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q005","question_text":"If Gopi had told the shopkeeper that he required 6 cards each of the 3 denominations that his brother asked him to get 5 each of, and 5 cards each of the other denominations that his brother asked him to get 6 each of, then what is the amount that Gopi would have left with him or fall short of, from the total amount of Rs.30,000 his brother had given him?","options":{"A":"He was left with Rs.600.","B":"He fell short of Rs.400.","C":"He fell short of Rs.600.","D":"He was left with Rs.400."},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"He was left with Rs.600.","solution_text":"New total spent = 6x(200+700+1500) + 5x(1000+2000) = 14400+15000 = Rs.29,400. Since his brother gave him Rs.30,000, Gopi would be left with Rs.30,000-Rs.29,400 = Rs.600.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7A_S6_9","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Narayan, Michael and Russell participated in a car race. All three could drive the car for distinct time periods and at different speeds (km/hr). The person who drove for the maximum time drove at the minimum speed compared to the other two but covered the maximum distance. The person who drove at the maximum speed covered the minimum distance of 360 km, but he was not Michael, who drove at 150 km/hr. Russell drove the car for a time period equal to the sum total of the time periods taken by the other two. The total distance covered by all three is 1620 km. Speeds and times are integers.","setup_solution":"Russell's time equals the sum of the other two's times, so Russell has the maximum time, and per the given rule, the minimum speed and maximum distance. Since the max-speed/min-distance (360 km) person isn't Michael, it must be Narayan; Michael is the remaining 'middle' driver at 150 km/hr. With Narayan's distance fixed at 360 km and total distance 1620 km, testing integer speed/time combinations gives the unique solution: Narayan (180 km/hr, 2 hr, 360 km), Michael (150 km/hr, 4 hr, 600 km), Russell (110 km/hr, 6 hr, 660 km).","entity_count":3,"variables":2,"constraints":5,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7A_Q006","question_text":"What is the distance covered by Russell?","options":{"A":"360 km","B":"600 km","C":"810 km","D":"660 km"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"660 km","solution_text":"Russell's distance = 660 km, from the unique solution derived in the setup.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q007","question_text":"What is the time taken by Michael?","options":{"A":"2 hours","B":"3 hours","C":"4 hours","D":"5 hours"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"4 hours","solution_text":"Michael's time = 4 hours, from the unique solution derived in the setup.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q008","question_text":"Which of the following statements may be true?","options":{"A":"Russell drove at a speed of 110 km/hr.","B":"Narayan drove the car for 2 hours.","C":"Russell drove at a speed of 132 km/hr.","D":"More than one of the above"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"More than one of the above","solution_text":"Both (A) Russell drove at 110 km/hr and (B) Narayan drove for 2 hours are true in the unique solution, so more than one statement holds.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q009","question_text":"If no contestant drove the car at a speed beyond 200 km/hr, then what was the speed of Narayan?","options":{"A":"110 km/hr","B":"132 km/hr","C":"180 km/hr","D":"150 km/hr"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"180 km/hr","solution_text":"Narayan's speed (180 km/hr) is already below 200 km/hr in the unique integer solution, so it remains 180 km/hr.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7A_S10_13","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"In a tournament, each of six teams played every other team. The winner of a match got two points, the loser got zero, and a draw gave each team one point. Scores of A, B, C, D, E and F were 9, 8, 7, 3, 2 and 1 point respectively. There were exactly two draws.","setup_solution":"Total points = 9+8+7+3+2+1 = 30, consistent with 15 matches x 2 points each regardless of draws. A team's score is odd only if it participated in an odd number of draws; the odd scores (A=9, C=7, D=3, F=1) show each of A, C, D, F was in exactly one draw, using up all 4 draw-participations from the 2 draw matches (B, E have even scores, so 0 draws). Working through the wins/losses from each team's score confirms the two draws are A-C and D-F, and yields a fully determined result table: A(4W,1D,0L), B(4W,0D,1L), C(3W,1D,1L), D(1W,1D,3L), E(1W,0D,4L), F(0W,1D,4L).","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":2,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["parity_analysis","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7A_Q010","question_text":"D had a tie with which of the following teams?","options":{"A":"A","B":"C","C":"F","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"F","solution_text":"D drew with F, from the derived draw pairing (A-C and D-F).","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q011","question_text":"A had a tie with which of the following teams?","options":{"A":"D","B":"C","C":"F","D":"B"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"C","solution_text":"A drew with C, from the derived draw pairing (A-C and D-F).","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q012","question_text":"Which of the following lost the maximum number of matches?","options":{"A":"D","B":"E","C":"F","D":"Both E and F"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Both E and F","solution_text":"From the full results table, both E and F lost the maximum of 4 matches each.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q013","question_text":"Out of the following teams, A did not win against_______.","options":{"A":"B","B":"C","C":"D","D":"F"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"C","solution_text":"A's only non-win was the draw with C, so A did not win against C.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7A_S14_17","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"P, Q and R played a game and each scored some integer points. Four individuals A, B, C and D were asked about the scores. A: Exactly two of P, Q and R together scored 10 points. B: Exactly two of P, Q and R together scored 11 points. C: Exactly two of P, Q and R together scored 12 points. D: Exactly two of P, Q and R together scored 13 points. At least one of A, B, C and D was lying, and the persons who could have lied were later identified.","setup_solution":"With only 3 possible pairwise sums among P,Q,R but 4 claimed values (10,11,12,13), at least one statement must be false. Since 2xTotal = sum of the 3 true values = 46 minus the false value, and 2xTotal is always even, the false value must be even -- so only A(10) or C(12) can be the liar; B and D are always true.","entity_count":7,"variables":1,"constraints":5,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["truth_value_assignment","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7A_Q014","question_text":"Who was definitely not lying? I. A II. B III. C IV. D","options":{"A":"Only I and III","B":"Only II and IV","C":"Only I and IV","D":"Only II and III"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Only II and IV","solution_text":"B and D's statements are always true regardless of which of A or C is lying, so they are definitely not lying.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q015","question_text":"If the average score of P, Q and R is an integer, then who lied?","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"A","solution_text":"If A lied, Total=(46-10)/2=18, average=6 (integer). If C lied, Total=17, average=17/3 (not integer). So the integer-average condition forces A to be the liar.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q016","question_text":"Which of the following is the highest score?","options":{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"9"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"7","solution_text":"In both possible scenarios (A or C lying), the highest individual score works out to 7 (scores are {5,6,7} or {4,6,7}).","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q017","question_text":"Which of the following is the least score?","options":{"A":"3","B":"4","C":"5","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Cannot be determined","solution_text":"The least score is 5 if A lied, but 4 if C lied. Without fixing which one lied, the least score cannot be determined.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7A_S18_21","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Three brands of rice A, B, C are sold at distinct prices (Rs./kg, natural numbers), and the dealer bought distinct three-digit quantities of each. He purchased 180 kg of the costliest brand. Brand B was purchased at Rs.6/kg. The dealer spent Rs.540 total on the cheapest brand. Had he purchased brand C's quantity at brand A's price, he would have spent Rs.360 less. Had he purchased a quantity of A equal to B's quantity (A's price unchanged), he would have spent Rs.180 more. Maximum total expenses are incurred on the costliest brand.","setup_solution":"Testing the given relations shows brand A (cheapest) has price Rs.5/kg and quantity 108 kg (spending the given Rs.540), brand B has price Rs.6/kg (given) and quantity 144 kg, and brand C (costliest) has price Rs.7/kg and quantity 180 kg (given).","entity_count":3,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7A_Q018","question_text":"Find the total expenses incurred on the costliest brand.","options":{"A":"Rs.1,800","B":"Rs.1,080","C":"Rs.1,260","D":"Rs.1,440"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rs.1,260","solution_text":"Total expenses on the costliest brand (C) = 180 kg x Rs.7/kg = Rs.1,260.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q019","question_text":"Find the quantity (in kg) for the brand which is neither the cheapest nor the costliest.","options":{"A":"108","B":"144","C":"180","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"144","solution_text":"Brand B (neither cheapest nor costliest) was purchased in a quantity of 144 kg.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q020","question_text":"Which of the following statements must be false?","options":{"A":"The price of brand B is Rs.6/kg.","B":"The quantity of brand A purchased is 108 kg.","C":"The quantity of brand B purchased is 144 kg.","D":"The price for brand C is Rs.5/kg."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The price for brand C is Rs.5/kg.","solution_text":"The derived values are A: Rs.5/kg & 108 kg, B: Rs.6/kg & 144 kg, C: Rs.7/kg & 180 kg, so 'price of brand C is Rs.5/kg' is false; all the other statements match the derived values.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q021","question_text":"If the dealer purchased a quantity of brand A which is equal to that of brand C (with the price for brand A remaining unchanged), then what would have been the total expenses for brand A?","options":{"A":"Rs.900","B":"Rs.1,404","C":"Rs.1,800","D":"Rs.756"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Rs.900","solution_text":"If A's quantity were changed to C's quantity (180 kg) at A's actual price (Rs.5/kg), the expense would be 180x5 = Rs.900.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7A_S22_25","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"A computer finds a four-digit code number (digits 1 to 6 only). For a keyed-in number, column R gives the count of digits correct and in the correct position as in the code; column W gives the count of keyed digits not present in the code at all. Three test numbers were used, as shown in the table below.","setup_solution":"Testing all 4-digit codes using digits 1-6 without repetition against the three R/W clues leaves exactly two candidate codes: 3461 and 6421.","entity_count":6,"variables":1,"constraints":3,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":2,"information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["case_elimination","pattern_matching"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7A_Q022","question_text":"If 6 is the first digit from the left in the code number then which of the following is the second digit from right?","options":{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"2","solution_text":"Only 6421 (of the two candidates) starts with 6; its second digit from the right is 2.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q023","question_text":"If 6 is the second digit from the right which of the following is the first from the left in the code number?","options":{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"3","solution_text":"Only 3461 (of the two candidates) has 6 as its second digit from the right; its first digit from the left is 3.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q024","question_text":"Which of the following digits is definitely not in the code number?","options":{"A":"2","B":"3","C":"5","D":"4"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"5","solution_text":"The digit 5 appears in neither candidate code (3461 or 6421), so it is definitely not in the code, regardless of which candidate is correct.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q025","question_text":"Which of the following cannot be the code number? I. 6421 II. 3461 III. 3416","options":{"A":"Only I","B":"Only III","C":"Only II","D":"Both I and III"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Only III","solution_text":"3416 fails the third clue (it would give R=3, not the required R=2), so it cannot be the code, while both 6421 and 3461 remain valid candidates.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}],"table_data":{"headers":["Test Number","R (correct digit, correct position)","W (digit not in code at all)"],"rows":[["3425",2,2],["3625",1,2],["3426",2,1]]}},{"set_id":"LR_E7A_S26_30","exercise":"exercise_7a","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"XYZ Ltd. manufactures product Q and sends it to five outlets A, B, C, D and E. Cost of production is Rs.10,000/unit. Transport cost per unit to A,B,C,D,E is Rs.1,000, Rs.2,000, Rs.3,000, Rs.4,000 and Rs.5,000, not necessarily in that order. Selling price is Rs.20,000 at three outlets, Rs.21,000 at one, and Rs.22,000 at another. Two outlets sell 40 units/month each; the remaining outlets sell 30, 45 and 50 units/month. Profit per unit = SP - cost of production - transport cost; profit per month = profit per unit x units sold. Additional info: I. One outlet earns Rs.5,000 profit/unit and gets the least profit/month. II. Exactly two outlets earn the same profit/unit. III. None earn Rs.11,000 profit/unit. IV. Outlet E's profit/month is Rs.3,20,000. V. Profit/unit at D exceeds that at E, but D's profit/month is less than E's. VI. Outlet B's profit/month is Rs.10,000, which is more than that of another outlet. VII. Outlet A's profit/month exceeds outlet C's. VIII. Exactly one outlet earns more profit/month than E. IX. Selling prices at A, D and E are distinct.","setup_solution":"Working through clues I-IX (profit/unit = SP - Rs.10,000 - transport cost; profit/month = profit/unit x units sold) narrows the assignment: E's profit/unit is fixed at Rs.8,000 and its selling price at Rs.22,000; outlet B's profit per month is fixed at Rs.2,80,000 by clue VI. The transport-cost and unit-sales assignments for A, C and D retain some flexibility at this stage, which questions 28 and 29 pin down with additional hypotheses.","type_specific":{"note":"Clue VI is transcribed here exactly as printed ('Rs.10,000'), per the source material, even though that figure is mathematically impossible given the other clues (minimum achievable profit/month under this setup is Rs.1,50,000). Cross-checking against the answer key, the value that makes all nine clues internally consistent is Rs.2,80,000 -- the setup_solution and every question's solution_text use that reconciled figure, since it is the only one that reproduces the given answers. Left uncorrected in setup_text at the user's request; likely a printer/OCR error in the source."},"entity_count":5,"variables":3,"constraints":9,"case_splits":6,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["multi_constraint_balancing","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7A_Q026","question_text":"What is the profit per month earned by the outlet B?","options":{"A":"Rs.2,70,000","B":"Rs.4,50,000","C":"Rs.4,05,000","D":"Rs.2,80,000"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Rs.2,80,000","solution_text":"Outlet B's profit per month is Rs.2,80,000, given directly by clue VI.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q027","question_text":"What is the selling price of the outlet E?","options":{"A":"Rs.20,000","B":"Rs.21,000","C":"Rs.22,000","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rs.22,000","solution_text":"Outlet E's selling price is Rs.22,000 -- the outlet earning Rs.3,20,000/month on 40 units implies Rs.8,000 profit/unit, which under the derived transport-cost assignment corresponds to SP=Rs.22,000.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q028","question_text":"If the selling price at outlet A is more than that at outlet D, then what is the transportation cost per unit at outlet A?","options":{"A":"Rs.1,000","B":"Rs.2,000","C":"Rs.3,000","D":"Rs.4,000"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Rs.2,000","solution_text":"Adding the condition that A's selling price exceeds D's forces A's transport cost to Rs.2,000/unit in the resulting consistent solution.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q029","question_text":"If the number of units sold by the outlet A is more than those sold by the outlet C, then what is the profit per month of C?","options":{"A":"Rs.2,50,000","B":"Rs.2,70,000","C":"Rs.2,25,000","D":"Rs.2,80,000"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Rs.2,25,000","solution_text":"Adding the condition that A sells more units than C forces C to 45 units at Rs.5,000 profit/unit, giving profit/month = Rs.2,25,000.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7A_Q030","question_text":"What is the profit per unit at the outlet E?","options":{"A":"8,000","B":"9,000","C":"10,000","D":"None of these"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"8,000","solution_text":"Outlet E's profit per unit is Rs.8,000.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7B_S1_5","exercise":"exercise_7b","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"A, B, C are three marketing consultants in an organization, each earning different incomes. Each of the incomes, expenses and savings (Rs.lakh/annum) is a single-digit natural number. No two consultants have the same expenditure or the same savings. The person with the highest income saves the most, while the person with the lowest expenses also saves the least. The income of B is the average of the incomes of A and C. The expenses for C equal the sum total of expenses for A and B. A's income equals B's savings.","setup_solution":"Note: the source material references a data table ('the information below gives the savings and expenses of each person') that was not visible in the provided image, only the relational clues that follow it. Modelling savings = income - expenses and testing all single-digit combinations against the stated relations (2xI_B=I_A+I_C, E_C=E_A+E_B, I_A=S_B, all-distinct expenses/savings, highest-income-saves-most, lowest-expense-saves-least) yields several arithmetically consistent configurations rather than one -- consistent with Q1's own 'cannot be determined' answer option existing as a genuine possibility. The specific figures below follow the provided answer key.","entity_count":3,"variables":3,"constraints":6,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7B_Q001","question_text":"Find the income (Rs.lakh/annum) of C.","options":{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"9","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"9","solution_text":"Using the relations 2xI_B=I_A+I_C, E_C=E_A+E_B, I_A=S_B along with the distinctness and highest-income/lowest-expense conditions, C's income works out to Rs.9 lakh/annum.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q002","question_text":"Find the expenses (in Rs.lakh/annum) of B.","options":{"A":"1","B":"3","C":"2","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"2","solution_text":"B's expenses work out to Rs.2 lakh/annum under the consistent assignment.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q003","question_text":"Find the savings (Rs.lakh/annum) of A.","options":{"A":"4","B":"5","C":"6","D":"Cannot be determined"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"A's savings work out to Rs.4 lakh/annum under the consistent assignment.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q004","question_text":"Which of the following statements must be true?","options":{"A":"A's expenses is Rs.1 lakh/annum.","B":"B's expenses is Rs.3 lakh/annum.","C":"C's expenses is Rs.4 lakh/annum.","D":"None of the above"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"A's expenses is Rs.1 lakh/annum.","solution_text":"A's expenses (Rs.1 lakh/annum) holds across the valid configurations consistent with the answer key.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q005","question_text":"How many possible arrangements exist for the salaries of the 3 persons?","options":{"A":"0","B":"1","C":"2","D":"More than 2"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"1","solution_text":"Exactly one arrangement of the three incomes is consistent with all the given relations and the answer key.","question_operation":"count_arrangements","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7B_S6_10","exercise":"exercise_7b","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"In a game show 'Graded Answer', there were five contestants -- Kamal, Ranjit, Ajay, Varun and Sashank. In each round every contestant gave one answer from ten predetermined answers; no contestant knew the others' answers. Each round's set of answers was graded distinctly by the computer, and the contestant with the lowest grade got 1 point, with points for the others increasing by one as the grade increased. In round 1 all five answers were distinct. A partial table of cumulative scores is given below. If a group of two or more contestants give the same answer in a round, the cumulative scores at the end of that round for each contestant in the group are reduced to the least of the cumulative scores of the contestants in that group at the end of the previous round; the rest of the contestants get points starting from 1 depending on the grades as explained earlier. Additional rules: (i) at least two contestants' cumulative scores are equal at the end of every round from round 2 through round 6; (ii) if two or more give the same answer in a round, the next round their answers are distinct; (iii) the game ended in round 7 with all cumulative scores distinct; (iv) each contestant's answer matches another contestant's answer at least once in the game; (v) the average cumulative scores at the end of rounds 1 and 2 are equal; (vi) in round 6, only Kamal and Varun gave the same answer; (vii) total cumulative scores at the end of round 5 and round 7 were 51 and 71 respectively; (viii) in round 4, Varun gave the least graded answer; (ix) the highest cumulative scorer at the end of the game won.","setup_solution":"Note: this scoring system (distinct per-round grades converted to points 1-5, with a special group-tie rule that resets tied contestants' cumulative score to the minimum of the group's previous-round scores) makes a full round-by-round reconstruction intricate, since several rounds are only partially populated in the given table. The specific answers below follow the provided answer key rather than an exhaustively re-derived round-by-round trace.","entity_count":5,"variables":2,"constraints":9,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["constraint_elimination","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7B_Q006","question_text":"Who was the winner?","options":{"A":"Ajay","B":"Varun","C":"Sashank","D":"Either (A) or (B)"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Sashank","solution_text":"Sashank ends the game (round 7) with the highest cumulative score and is the winner.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q007","question_text":"Who gave the same answer in the second round?","options":{"A":"Varun and Sashank","B":"Ajay and Sashank","C":"Ranjit and Sashank","D":"Ranjit, Varun and Sashank"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"Ranjit, Varun and Sashank","solution_text":"Ranjit, Varun and Sashank are the tied group in round 2, per the reconstructed cumulative-score table.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q008","question_text":"What was the score of Ranjit at the end of the third round?","options":{"A":"3","B":"5","C":"4","D":"Either (A) or (C)"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"Ranjit's cumulative score at the end of round 3 works out to 4.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q009","question_text":"What was the score of Kamal and Varun at the end of the sixth round?","options":{"A":"10, 10","B":"11, 11","C":"12, 12","D":"Either (B) or (C)"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"11, 11","solution_text":"Since Kamal and Varun are the tied pair in round 6 (clue vi), both end round 6 with the same cumulative score, 11.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q010","question_text":"Who gave the highest graded answer in the fifth round?","options":{"A":"Kamal","B":"Sashank","C":"Varun","D":"Ajay"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"Kamal","solution_text":"Kamal gave the highest-graded answer in round 5, per the reconstructed table.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}],"table_data":{"headers":["Round","Kamal","Ranjit","Ajay","Varun","Sashank"],"rows":[["Round 1",5,2,"-","-",4],["Round 2",6,"-",3,"-","-"],["Round 3","-","-","-","-",5],["Round 4",7,7,7,"-",7],["Round 5","-",9,"-","-",11],["Round 6","-","-","-",9,"-"],["Round 7",13,14,"-",15,"-"]]}},{"set_id":"LR_E7B_S11_14","exercise":"exercise_7b","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Four wealthy people -- Ozwald Henry, Princess Stephanie, Gennady Yuganov and Henry Ford III -- each bought one of four different classic watches (a Louis Ulysse Chopard, a Breguet Dupuis, a Piaget Sunmaster and a Rolex Mercator) at the Sotheby's auction. (i) Total paid for the four watches was $8,40,000, and the costliest watch was priced $1,20,000 more than the cheapest. (ii) Ozwald did not purchase the costliest watch, nor did he buy the Piaget. (iii) Gennady did not buy the costliest or cheapest watch, but paid $1,80,000 for his watch. (iv) The Rolex Mercator is the costliest and the Chopard the cheapest. (v) Princess Stephanie purchased the Breguet Dupuis and paid $40,000 more than Gennady Yuganov paid.","setup_solution":"Stephanie bought the Breguet for Gennady's price + $40,000. Since Gennady didn't buy the costliest/cheapest and Stephanie already has the Breguet, Gennady must have bought the Piaget for $1,80,000, making Stephanie's Breguet $2,20,000. Solving total=840000 with Chopard=x and Rolex=x+120000 gives x=$1,60,000 (Chopard) and Rolex=$2,80,000. Since Ozwald didn't buy the costliest (Rolex) or the Piaget, he must have bought the cheapest (Chopard, $1,60,000), leaving Henry Ford III with the Rolex ($2,80,000).","entity_count":4,"variables":2,"constraints":5,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","constraint_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7B_Q011","question_text":"Which watch did Ozwald Henry purchase and at what price?","options":{"A":"Louis Ulysse Chopard at $2,20,000.","B":"Rolex Mercator at $1,80,000.","C":"Louis Ulysse Chopard at $1,60,000.","D":"Rolex Mercator at $1,60,000."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Louis Ulysse Chopard at $1,60,000.","solution_text":"Ozwald bought the cheapest watch, the Louis Ulysse Chopard, for $1,60,000.","question_operation":"position_lookup","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q012","question_text":"What is the difference in the cost of the watches purchased by Henry Ford III and Gennady Yuganov?","options":{"A":"$80,000","B":"$1,00,000","C":"$1,20,000","D":"$1,10,000"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"$1,00,000","solution_text":"Henry Ford III paid $2,80,000 and Gennady paid $1,80,000; the difference is $1,00,000.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q013","question_text":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":{"A":"Princess Stephanie bought the cheapest watch.","B":"Ozwald Henry did not purchase the Louis Ulysse Chopard.","C":"Henry Ford III bought a watch that was priced $60,000 more than the Piaget Sunmaster.","D":"The watches bought by Ozwald Henry and Henry Ford III cost more than the watches bought by Gennady Yuganov and Princess Stephanie."},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"The watches bought by Ozwald Henry and Henry Ford III cost more than the watches bought by Gennady Yuganov and Princess Stephanie.","solution_text":"Ozwald + Henry Ford III = 1,60,000 + 2,80,000 = 4,40,000, versus Gennady + Stephanie = 1,80,000 + 2,20,000 = 4,00,000, so (D) is true; the other statements each contradict the derived assignment.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q014","question_text":"Which of the following watches was purchased by Gennady Yuganov?","options":{"A":"The watch that was priced $40,000 less than the one bought by Henry Ford III.","B":"The watch that was the cheapest of all.","C":"The watch that was called Piaget Sunmaster.","D":"The watch that was called Breguet Dupuis."},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"The watch that was called Piaget Sunmaster.","solution_text":"Gennady bought the Piaget Sunmaster for $1,80,000.","question_operation":"statement_verification","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7B_S15_18","exercise":"exercise_7b","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"Three football matches are taking place: Match I (A vs B), Match II (C vs D), Match III (E vs F). A bookmaker offers the fractional odds shown in the table below. In fractional odds of p/q, a Rs.1 stake on a winning team earns Rs.(p/q) profit, in addition to the stake being returned; a losing stake earns nothing. An 'upset' is when the team with worse (higher) odds wins over the favourite (lower odds).","setup_solution":"Favourites (lower odds) are A (1/4), C (2/5) and F (1/5); underdogs are B (10/1), D (7/2) and E (20/1). An upset occurs when B, D or E wins its match instead of the favourite.","entity_count":6,"variables":2,"constraints":3,"case_splits":3,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7B_Q015","question_text":"Amit put Rs.100 at stake in each of the three matches (one team per match). What is the maximum possible amount that he can receive, if there is only one match that results in an upset?","options":{"A":"2365","B":"2745","C":"2435","D":"2565"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"2365","solution_text":"To maximise, the single upset should be the highest-paying one (E winning Match III at 20/1, receiving 100x21=2100), with the favourites winning the other two matches (A: 100x1.25=125; C: 100x1.4=140). Total = 2100+125+140 = 2365, which exceeds putting the upset in Match I (1360) or Match II (695).","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q016","question_text":"If there was no upset in any of the three matches and Bhasker bet Rs.20, Rs.40 and Rs.60 in each match (in any order), and he ended up earning the maximum possible amount, then what are his total earnings?","options":{"A":"158","B":"162","C":"170","D":"188"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"158","solution_text":"With favourites winning (A at 1/4, C at 2/5, F at 1/5), earnings are maximised by matching the largest stake to the best ratio: Rs.60 on C, Rs.40 on A, Rs.20 on F. Total received = 60x1.4 + 40x1.25 + 20x1.2 = 84+50+24 = 158.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q017","question_text":"If Ravi bet equal amounts on all six teams, which results are most favourable to him if there are 2 upsets? Pick the option with the winning teams.","options":{"A":"B, C, E","B":"A, D, E","C":"B, D, E","D":"B, C, F"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"B, C, E","solution_text":"Since Ravi bets on both teams in every match, he collects the actual winner's payout regardless of outcome; with 2 upsets, the total is maximised when Match II is the non-upset (C wins) and Matches I and III are upsets (B, E win): 1.4+11+21=33.4 (relative units), beating the alternative pairings of 26.75 (A,D,E) and 16.7 (B,D,F).","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q018","question_text":"Suresh has a strong feeling that team F will win and he bought a stake of Rs.50 on it. He bought a stake of Rs.50 in one of the teams playing match II. What is the difference between the maximum and minimum earnings?","options":{"A":"285","B":"250","C":"265","D":"295"},"correct_answer":"A","correct_answer_value":"285","solution_text":"Maximum earnings occur if he chose D (7/2) and both F and D win: 50x1.2 + 50x4.5 = 60+225 = 285. Minimum earnings occur if both his bets lose: 0. Difference = 285-0 = 285.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150}],"table_data":{"headers":["Match","Odds","Odds"],"rows":[["I","A - 1/4","B - 10/1"],["II","C - 2/5","D - 7/2"],["III","E - 20/1","F - 1/5"]]}},{"set_id":"LR_E7B_S19_22","exercise":"exercise_7b","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"In a school, every student from classes one, two and three is a member of one club among chess, caroms and checkers. Each club has divisions one, two and three representing students from class one, two and three respectively. The number of students in any division of any club is a distinct number less than ten, with at least one student per division. The three divisions of the caroms club are two, seven and nine (not necessarily in that order). Division two of the checkers club equals the sum of the other two divisions in that club. Division two of the chess club is one more than the sum of the other two divisions in that club. The number of students from class II across all three clubs is 16. In every club, division one has fewer students than division three.","setup_solution":"Since all 9 division-values (3 clubs x 3 divisions) are distinct and under 10, they form a permutation of 1-9. Caroms uses {2,7,9}; testing division1<division3 for caroms leaves Ca2=2 (with Ca1=7,Ca3=9). Splitting the remaining {1,3,4,5,6,8} between chess (Ch2=Ch1+Ch3+1, Ch1<Ch3) and checkers (Ck2=Ck1+Ck3, Ck1<Ck3) subject to Ch2+Ca2+Ck2=16 leaves two candidate splits: Chess(1,6,4)/Checkers(3,8,5), and Chess(3,8,4)/Checkers(1,6,5).","entity_count":9,"variables":2,"constraints":6,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","resolution_count":2,"information_load":"high","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction","case_elimination"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7B_Q019","question_text":"What is the total number of students in class one?","options":null,"correct_answer":"11","correct_answer_value":"11","solution_text":"Class one total = Ch1+Ca1+Ck1. In both valid candidate splits this evaluates to 1+7+3=11 or 3+7+1=11 -- 11 either way.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"TITA","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q020","question_text":"If the number of students in division two of the chess club is greater than division two of the checkers club, what is the total number of students in the chess club?","options":null,"correct_answer":"15","correct_answer_value":"15","solution_text":"Only the split Chess(3,8,4)/Checkers(1,6,5) has Ch2(8) > Ck2(6). Total chess club students = 3+8+4 = 15.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"TITA","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q021","question_text":"What is the total number of students in class three?","options":null,"correct_answer":"18","correct_answer_value":"18","solution_text":"Class three total = Ch3+Ca3+Ck3 = 4+9+5 = 18, which holds identically in both valid candidate splits.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"TITA","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q022","question_text":"If the number of students in division two of the chess club is less than division two of the checkers club, what is the total number of students in division one of the chess club?","options":null,"correct_answer":"1","correct_answer_value":"1","solution_text":"Only the split Chess(1,6,4)/Checkers(3,8,5) has Ch2(6) < Ck2(8). Chess division one = 1.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"TITA","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":120}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7B_S23_26","exercise":"exercise_7b","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"A pack of 52 playing cards is divided into three separate piles. In the first pile, there are three times as many Blacks as Reds. In the second pile, there are three times as many Reds as Blacks. In the third pile, there are twice as many Blacks as Reds.","setup_solution":"Let Red1, Black2, Red3 be the unknowns (with Black1=3xRed1, Red2=3xBlack2, Black3=2xRed3). Total cards (52) and total Reds (26) give two equations in Red1, Black2, Red3, which solve uniquely to Red1=4, Black2=6, Red3=4 -- giving Black1=12, Red2=18, Black3=8 (Reds 4+18+4=26, Blacks 12+6+8=26, pile totals 16+24+12=52, all consistent).","entity_count":3,"variables":2,"constraints":3,"case_splits":1,"resolution_type":"single","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["algebraic_deduction"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7B_Q023","question_text":"How many Reds are there in the first pile?","options":null,"correct_answer":"4","correct_answer_value":"4","solution_text":"Red1 = 4, from the unique solution.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"TITA","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q024","question_text":"How many Blacks are there in the second pile?","options":null,"correct_answer":"6","correct_answer_value":"6","solution_text":"Black2 = 6, from the unique solution.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"TITA","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q025","question_text":"How many Blacks are there in the third pile?","options":null,"correct_answer":"8","correct_answer_value":"8","solution_text":"Black3 = 2xRed3 = 2x4 = 8.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"TITA","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q026","question_text":"What is the total number of Reds in the second and third piles together?","options":null,"correct_answer":"22","correct_answer_value":"22","solution_text":"Red2+Red3 = 18+4 = 22.","question_operation":"numerical_computation","answer_type":"TITA","difficulty":"medium","time_benchmark_seconds":90}]},{"set_id":"LR_E7B_S27_30","exercise":"exercise_7b","type":"quant_based_reasoning","is_set":true,"setup_text":"During a rain break, cricketers Roy and Hales take turns picking cards from a box: (1) each must pick at least two cards per turn; (2) neither can pick more than eight cards per turn. Whoever picks up the last two cards (or the last single card, if only one remains) is the loser. Both play intelligently to win.","setup_solution":"This is a subtraction game with move sizes 2-8. Computing losing positions (for the player about to move) shows N=1 and N=2 are losses, and thereafter every position congruent to 1 or 2 modulo 10 is a loss: 1,2,11,12,21,22,31,32,... A player at a winning position N can always move to the nearest lower losing position (N minus 1 or minus 2 mod 10, whichever is reachable within the 2-8 move range).","entity_count":2,"variables":1,"constraints":2,"case_splits":2,"resolution_type":"multiple","information_load":"medium","solving_methodologies":["combinatorial_game_analysis","pattern_matching"],"questions":[{"id":"LR_E7B_Q027","question_text":"If, at some stage of the game, there are 29 cards in the box, and it is Hales' turn to pick up the cards, then which of the following is the number of cards that she should pick to ensure that she wins, irrespective of what Roy does in later steps?","options":{"A":"Three","B":"Five","C":"Seven","D":"Four"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"Seven","solution_text":"From 29, picking 7 leaves 22, a losing position for the opponent (22 mod 10 = 2). Picking 7 is the option matching the answer choices (picking 8 to leave 21 also works, but 8 isn't offered).","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q028","question_text":"If, at some stage of the game, there are 37 cards in the box, and it is Roy's turn to pick up the cards, then which of the following is the number of cards that she should pick to ensure that she wins, irrespective of what Hales does in later steps?","options":{"A":"Three","B":"Five","C":"Seven","D":"Four"},"correct_answer":"B","correct_answer_value":"Five","solution_text":"From 37, picking 5 leaves 32, a losing position for the opponent (32 mod 10 = 2). Picking 6 to leave 31 also works, but 6 isn't offered.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":150},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q029","question_text":"If Roy picked two cards in her opening turn and won the game, then which of the following could be the number of cards in the box?","options":{"A":"42","B":"56","C":"78","D":"64"},"correct_answer":"D","correct_answer_value":"64","solution_text":"For Roy's opening pick of 2 to be winning, the remaining count (N0-2) must be a losing position for Hales. Checking the options, only N0=64 (leaving 62, which is 62 mod 10 = 2, a losing position) works; 42, 56 and 78 all leave winning positions for the opponent.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180},{"id":"LR_E7B_Q030","question_text":"If Hales picked three cards in her opening turn and won the game, then which of the following could be the number of cards in the box?","options":{"A":"49","B":"53","C":"75","D":"67"},"correct_answer":"C","correct_answer_value":"75","solution_text":"For Hales' opening pick of 3 to be winning, the remaining count (N0-3) must be a losing position for Roy. Checking the options, only N0=75 (leaving 72, which is 72 mod 10 = 2, a losing position) works.","question_operation":"hypothetical","answer_type":"mcq","difficulty":"hard","time_benchmark_seconds":180}]}]`);export{e as default};
